92186049236 #edb/ref 1034 0.7370391399213817 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186049236 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "e5b028a3-9410-51df-925f-0bb38d65ee4b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049934 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049111 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049934 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186092386 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049934 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186092387 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049934 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186092388 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049934 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186092389 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049934 #edb/ref 1031 "Estimating Derivatives Using a Forward Difference Quotient" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049934 #edb/ref 1033 992 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186049934 #edb/ref 1034 1.0729976074514382 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186049934 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "1fbdb06d-f1f4-55f3-bf00-af01b659732d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078208 #edb/ref 1069 "e-158" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078208 #edb/ref 1071 "A function $f(x)$ has values according to the table below:\n\n| **$x$** | $0$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ | $0.3$ | $0.4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $1$ | $1.1$ | $1.3$ | $1.7$ | $2.5$ |\n\nApproximate $f'(x)$ at the point $x=0.2$ using a central difference approximation." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078208 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "98fdea06-a5ad-5d22-b928-ef893424e447" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078208 #edb/ref 1074 "We use the central difference approximation\n\n$$\nf'(x) \\approx \\frac{f(x+h) - f(x-h)}{2h}\n$$\n\n \nat the point $x=0.2.$ We choose the step size $h$ to be as small as possible, in this case $h=0.1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(0.2) & ≈\\frac{f(0.2+0.1)-f(0.2-0.1)}{2(0.1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(0.3)-f(0.1)}{2(0.1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1.7-1.1}{0.2} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.6}{0.2} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{2} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078209 #edb/ref 1069 "e-159" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078209 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/53/5355859a0307e46ae84d12bc5e0b183d72458972c8a417fb6d9296b4769ea078.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $y=g(x)$ is shown above. \nApproximate $g'(x)$ at the point $x=4$ using a central difference approximation with step size $h=4.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078209 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "62934fda-d972-50d5-acdb-e1f7b2fd63f2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078209 #edb/ref 1074 "We use the central difference approximation\n\n$$\ng'(x) \\approx \\dfrac{g(x+h) - g(x-h)}{2h}\n$$\n\n \n\nat the point $x=4$ with step size $h=4.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(4) & ≈\\frac{g(4+4)-g(4-4)}{2(4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{g(8)-g(0)}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4-(-10)}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{8} \\\\  & =0.75.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078212 #edb/ref 1069 "e-160" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078212 #edb/ref 1071 "A car engine is being heated from a temperature of about $-10^{\\circ}.$ The temperature of the engine after $t$ minutes is given by $T(t).$  The graph of $T(t)$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/fe/fe78a78911e2df9cdb488ec57b0e0a34bb56e18b57303d895dc8013b64a4afc3.png)\n\nApproximate the rate at which the temperature of the engine increases when $t = 3$ using a central difference approximation with step size $h=1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078212 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d405fda1-1111-56db-ae34-4c0f257852f2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078212 #edb/ref 1074 "We use the central difference approximation\n\n$$\nT'(t) \\approx \\dfrac{T(t+h) - T(t-h)}{2h},\n$$\n\n \n\nat the time $t=3$ with step size $h=1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(3) & ≈\\frac{T(3+1)-T(3-1)}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{T(4)-T(2)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{60-30}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{30}{2} \\\\  & =15.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of temperature increase is approximately $15^{\\circ}$ per minute." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078606 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049236 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078606 #edb/ref 1037 #e