n\n${f}^{′}(1.5)≈$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508034 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0089d6b7-bea1-5c5c-b759-cd65107e82f0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508034 #edb/ref 1074 "We use the central difference approximation\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)≈\\frac{f(x+h)-f(x-h)}{2h}\n$$\n\n \nat the point $x=1.5$ with the step size $h=0.25.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ≈\\frac{f(x+h)-f(x-h)}{2h} \\\\ {f}^{′}(1.5) & ≈\\frac{f(1.5+0.25)-f(1.5-0.25)}{2(0.25)} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(1.75)-f(1.25)}{0.5} \\\\  & =\\frac{-0.50-0.75}{0.5} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-1.25)}{0.5} \\\\  & =-2.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508037 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508026 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508037 #edb/ref 1069 "q-314885" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508037 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508037 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $f(1.9)=4.21$ and $f(2.1)=5.81,$ find an approximation for ${f}^{′}(2).$\n\n${f}^{′}(2)≈$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508037 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "02a39903-e9f1-5373-88d1-5e7a858c1c6d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508037 #edb/ref 1074 "We use the central difference approximation\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)≈\\frac{f(x+h)-f(x-h)}{2h}\n$$\n\n \nat the point $x=2$ with the step size $h=0.1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ≈\\frac{f(x+h)-f(x-h)}{2h} \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & ≈\\frac{f(2+0.1)-f(2-0.1)}{2(0.1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(2.1)-f(1.9)}{0.2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5.81-4.21}{0.2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1.6}{0.2} \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508062 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186507961 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508062 #edb/ref 1069 "q-344364" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508062 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508062 #edb/ref 1071 "In a nutrition study, the mass of bacteria in a culture (in $\\text{g}$) after $t$ hours is given by the function $M(t).$ The values of $M(t)$ for different values of $t$ are given in the table below:\n\n| **$t$** | $1.5$ | $1.8$ | $2.1$ | $2.4$ | $2.7$ | $3.0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **$M(t)$** | $6.2$ | $6.8$ | $7.5$ | $8.1$ | $8.7$ | $9.3$ |\n\nApproximate the bacteria’s mass instantaneous rate of change at $t=2.4$ using a central difference approximation with step size $h=0.3.$\n\nThe bacteria’s mass at $t=2.4$ is changing at approximately \n{{field-1}} $g$ per hour." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508062 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5bf64a24-1ed0-5d2f-8ee9-666d256fd892" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508062 #edb/ref 1074 "We use the central difference approximation\n\n$$\n{M}^{′}(t)≈\\frac{M(t+h)-M(t-h)}{2h},\n$$\n\nat time $t=2.4$ with step size $h=0.3.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}^{′}(2.4) & ≈\\frac{M(2.4+0.3)-M(2.4-0.3)}{2(0.3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{M(2.7)-M(2.1)}{0.6} \\\\  & =\\frac{8.7-7.5}{0.6} \\\\  & =\\frac{1.2}{0.6} \\\\  & =2.0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the bacteria’s mass at $t=2.4$ is changing at approximately $2.0\\,g$ per hour." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508065 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186507990 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508065 #edb/ref 1069 "q-4809" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508065 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508065 #edb/ref 1071 "Given the following data for a function $f(x):$\n\n| **$x$** | $1.1$ | $1.3$ | $1.5$ | $1.7$ | $1.9$ | $2.1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $12$ | $15$ | $21$ | $23$ | $24$ | $25$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nApproximate ${f}^{′}(x)$ at the point $x=1.7$ using a central difference approximation." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508065 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "61df167c-8856-5b89-8c68-3c6ad59fc5b1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508065 #edb/ref 1074 "We use the central difference approximation\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)≈\\frac{f(x+h)-f(x-h)}{2h}\n$$\n\n \nat the point $x=1.7.$ We choose the step size $h$ to be as small as possible, in this case $h=0.2.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(1.7) & ≈\\frac{f(1.7+0.2)-f(1.7-0.2)}{2(0.2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(1.9)-f(1.5)}{2(0.2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{24-21}{0.4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{0.4} \\\\  & =7.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508066 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186507958 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508066 #edb/ref 1069 "q-47498" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508066 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508066 #edb/ref 1071 "A car travels on a straight road in such a way that its position, in meters, after $t$ seconds is given by the function $f(t).$ The values of $f(t)$ for different values of $t$ are given in the table below:\n\n| **$t$** | $2$ | $4$ | $6$ | $8$ | $10$ | $12$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(t)$ | $1$ | $2$ | $4$ | $6$ | $14$ | $15$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nApproximate the velocity ${f}^{′}(t)$ at the point $t=4$ using a central difference approximation with step size $h=2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508066 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "630319b1-f66a-5f1d-ad45-2673438230d6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508066 #edb/ref 1074 "We use the central difference approximation\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(t)≈\\frac{f(t+h)-f(t-h)}{2h},\n$$\n\nat the time $t=4$ with step size $h=2.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(4) & ≈\\frac{f(4+2)-f(4-2)}{2(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(6)-f(2)}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{4-1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\  & =0.75.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity of the car at the instant of time $t=4$ is $0.75\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508077 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508010 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508077 #edb/ref 1069 "q-47533" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508077 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508077 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/52/52d37029cc93ea1e0be1c9a1488aa3275437c4be2b749ff09a497935de8399a4.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $g(x)$ is shown above. Approximate ${g}^{′}(x)$ at the point $x=4$ using a central difference approximation with step size $h=2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508077 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "862b2bca-cb58-5918-b28a-220de43ef262" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508077 #edb/ref 1074 "We use the central difference approximation\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}(x)≈\\frac{g(x+h)-g(x-h)}{2h},\n$$\n\nat the point $x=4$ with step size $h=2.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(4) & ≈\\frac{g(4+2)-g(4-2)}{2(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{g(6)-g(2)}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{8-2}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{4} \\\\  & =1.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508079 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186507985 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508079 #edb/ref 1069 "q-344003" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508079 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508079 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/e5/e526d91e13619efd7dfd8cab4143a43a4e0d373ad2766a0aac2de02a8f7ec7f8.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Approximate ${f}^{′}(x)$ at the point $x=-2$ using a central difference approximation with step size $h=2.$\n\n*Hint: Assume that the graph passes through the points with integer $y$-coordinates when $x=-4$ and $x=0.$*\n\n${f}^{′}(-2)≈$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508079 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8a10d42c-0c01-597c-81cf-42c95eadf6ff" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508079 #edb/ref 1074 "We use the central difference approximation\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)≈\\frac{f(x+h)-f(x-h)}{2h}\n$$\n\n \nat the point $x=-2$ with step size $h=2.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-2) & ≈\\frac{f(-2+2)-f(-2-2)}{2(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(0)-f(-4)}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5-1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{4} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508086 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186507969 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508086 #edb/ref 1069 "q-344026" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508086 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508086 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/7c/7c2d36807b25464536f395912dad0443831f4846f23a337e9495cf673bd488d6.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Approximate ${f}^{′}(x)$ at the point $x=1$ using a central difference approximation with step size $h=1.$\n\n*Hint: Assume that the graph passes through the points with integer $y$-coordinates when $x=0$ and $x=2.$*\n\n${f}^{′}(1)≈$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508086 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9bc69072-1729-5358-9591-a855b171ce26" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508086 #edb/ref 1074 "We use the central difference approximation\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)≈\\frac{f(x+h)-f(x-h)}{2h},\n$$\n\nat the point $x=1$ with step size $h=1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(1) & ≈\\frac{f(1+1)-f(1-1)}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(2)-f(0)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3-(-1)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{2} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508103 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508005 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508103 #edb/ref 1069 "q-314288" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508103 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508103 #edb/ref 1071 "A function $g(x)$ has values according to the table below:\n\n| **$x$** | $0.6$ | $0.8$ | $1$ | $1.2$ | $1.4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $g(x)$ | $2.6$ | $1.9$ | $1.5$ | $2.3$ | $5.9$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nApproximate ${g}^{′}(x)$ at the point $x=0.8$ using a central difference approximation.\n\n${g}^{′}(0.8)≈$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508103 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d62032b2-c1a5-5a5d-886c-e5b808c77233" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508103 #edb/ref 1074 "We use the central difference approximation\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}(x)≈\\frac{g(x+h)-g(x-h)}{2h}\n$$\n\n \nat the point $x=0.8.$ We choose the step size $h$ to be as small as possible, in this case $h=0.2.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(0.8) & ≈\\frac{g(0.8+0.2)-g(0.8-0.2)}{2(0.2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{g(1)-g(0.6)}{2(0.2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1.5-2.6}{0.4} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1.1}{0.4} \\\\  & =\\frac{-11}{4} \\\\  & =-2.75.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508107 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508007 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508107 #edb/ref 1069 "q-51404" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508107 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508107 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/4e/4ede7ee4b2f41d19ba36d8e1cb28e9ab480a54c83310686baa35e7d08a7e7fd4.png)\n\nA liquid is heated to ${150}^{∘}\\text{C}$ and then left to cool. The temperature of the liquid after $t$ hours is given by $T(t).$  The graph of $T(t)$ is shown above.\n\nApproximate the rate of change of temperature when $t=2$ using a central difference approximation with step size $h=1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508107 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e2ff1bdc-524d-5a25-83f2-62b0e84aa209" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508107 #edb/ref 1074 "We use the central difference approximation\n\n$$\n{T}^{′}(x)≈\\frac{T(t+h)-T(t-h)}{2h},\n$$\n\n \nat the time $t=2$ and step size $h=1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(-1) & ≈\\frac{T(2+1)-T(2-1)}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{T(3)-T(1)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{60-120}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-60}{2} \\\\  & =-30.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of temperature at the instant of time $t=2$ is $-30{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C}$ per hour." #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
